SWEET Bluetooth Skywalk UART channel (patch 0286; design docs/research/bt-skywalk-uart-channel.md, sections 1-4). Stock bluetoothd opens the control "com.apple.uart.sk.bluetooth", calls setsockopt IOAOSSKYSETCHANNELSPEC (level SYSPROTO_CONTROL = 2, 0x800c5414, 12 bytes = u32 1024, 63, 63) and getsockopt IOAOSSKYGETCHANNELUUID (level 2, 0x40105412, 16 bytes), then os_channel_create(uuid, port 0) and does all H4 I/O through the channel's TX ring 0 / RX ring 2 (slot API, byte stream with the H4 type byte, packets may span slots) [bin: bluetoothd 0x100066f10..0x100067b90, read with capstone]. Until now tools/mkbtm49.py byte-patched that branch away. New bsd/dev/sweet_bt_sk.c (files entry in bsd/conf/files) serves it from the kernel: ONE persistent anonymous kernel-pipe nexus instance (default kpipe domain provider, 1 TX + 1 RX ring, 64 slots of 1024 bytes each; recipe if_utun.c utun_register_kernel_pipe_nexus / utun_enable_channel and nx_kpipe_loopback.c) created lazily at the first sk sockopt (the Skywalk core is not up at sweet_bt_init time [guess]); SETCHANNELSPEC logs the three u32 (a BT GAP line when they differ from 1024 63 63) and returns 0; GETCHANNELUUID returns the instance uuid. Provider callbacks: pre_connect fetches the packet pools (one channel at a time, a second gets EBUSY + GAP), ring_init/ring_fini keep the RX ring, connected switches the UART RX relay (sweet_bt_rx) from ctl_enqueuedata to a 64 KiB locked byte FIFO plus kern_channel_notify, pre_disconnect waits for in-flight notifiers (kern_channel_notify is never called under the FIFO lock because the kevent path takes the kq lock and then sync_rx), disconnected clears everything; sync_tx copies the slot buffers in slot order to the same sbt.ops->tx path the plain control uses (sweet_bt_sk_tx in sweet_bt.c, refused with ENOTCONN + GAP when the sk control is closed, ENXIO + GAP without a backend; a backend error is returned to bluetoothd after the remaining slots are consumed and counted as dropped, never silent); sync_rx fills slots from the FIFO (up to 1024 bytes each, kern_pbufpool_alloc_nosleep, bytes stay queued on allocation failure with a GAP). bluetoothd closes the control before it destroys the channel: the control close keeps the channel and drops unread RX bytes with a GAP. Boot-arg sweetbtsk=0 turns the channel off: the two sockopts are then not recognised and fail as before (ENOPROTOOPT + GAP) and the old "skywalk rows M12-M14" GAP line is printed; the plain control and BlueTool are unchanged. Every Skywalk call that fails is a BT GAP line with the errno and the partially built controller/provider is torn down in reverse order. No new hardware write: TX bytes take the existing UART path. Without SKYWALK/CONFIG_NEXUS_KERNEL_PIPE the file compiles to stubs that log a GAP. NOT run on hardware: unknowns are the Skywalk core on this kernel, the arena mmap into bluetoothd, kern_channel_notify from the RX thread, and whether a sandbox policy filters __channel_open (report s.4). Host test: tools/test_sweet_bt_sk.sh. Review/gap fix: once IOAOSSKYSETCHANNELSPEC succeeded on the sk control, UART RX goes to the channel FIFO (drop oldest with a BT GAP count when full) and is delivered when the channel connects; the FIFO is dropped with a GAP when the control closes first.
